<%# One day, on every screen that shows one.
    Locals: pick, chrome: — :front_door (/), :archive (/days/:date), :preview (curator),
    and current: — the pick the front door is showing, counted by the controller.

    The three modes differ in four places and nothing else:

      chrome:        masthead label      compass marks  rail keep     coda
      ------------   -----------------   -------------  ------------  ----------------------
      :front_door    Artwork of the Day  Today          yes           See you tomorrow. + the gallery
      :archive       From the archive    nothing        yes           prev · next · today
      :preview       Artwork of the Day  Today          NO            See you tomorrow. + the gallery

    An archived day marks nothing, because it is not one of the four surfaces — it is one
    day *inside* the archive, and marking `Days` there would unlink the only route back to
    the list. See ApplicationHelper#compass_destinations.

    :preview shows the reader's page, compass and all, because that is what a preview is
    for. It gets no keep control: the day is not published, so no reader can act on it, and
    a curator keeping unpublished works would be the first thing polluting the only usage
    signal that feature has. %>
<% painting = pick.painting
   src = artwork_src(painting)
   archive = chrome == :archive %>

<% content_for :title, "#{painting.title} — Tondo" %>
<% content_for :description, "#{painting.title} by #{painting.artist_display}, #{archive ? "from the Tondo archive" : "today's artwork on Tondo"}." %>
<% content_for :head do %>
  <link rel="preload" as="image" href="<%= src %>">
<% end %>

<%# The date used to be the door to the archive — a link, but only once there was
    more than one published day, and never labelled as anything but a date. The
    compass carries that door now, with the word `Days` on it and no condition,
    so this is a date again and nothing else. %>
<%= render "shared/masthead",
      label: archive ? "From the archive" : "Artwork of the Day",
      here: archive ? nil : :today,
      aside: pick.scheduled_on.to_fs(:daily),
      aside_datetime: pick.scheduled_on.iso8601 %>

<main class="page" data-controller="artwork">
  <%= render "shared/plate", painting: painting, eager: true,
        describedby: "daily-note", resting: "This work is resting — read the note." %>

  <%# The action rail. Under the plate, above the label — the position is the
      whole story: it does not move with the length of the note, so the keep
      control is on screen at open on a 60-word day and a 180-word one alike.
      Before this it was the last row of the wall label, which put it 40-60pt
      below the fold on a fallback day and several hundred below on a
      hand-written one, because rule 8 shows that note whole.
      `decisions/0010-actions-become-a-rail.md`.

      ZOOM FIRST, and not for emphasis. Keep and its count share one Turbo frame,
      and a frame is one contiguous flex item — when the count arrives the frame
      grows and shoves everything to its right, about 60pt, on every open, for
      exactly the reader who already has a collection. Putting the growing item
      last removes the shift by construction rather than reserving a width nobody
      can predict. Spreading the row instead is what DESIGN.md already refuses at
      the 680px measure.

      Everything public lives out here in the cached page; only the keep frame is
      per-visitor.

      The wrapper is conditional because both its children are. A curator
      previewing an unpublished work that has no picture gets neither control,
      and an empty `.rail` is not free: it still trips `.page:has(.rail)` and
      charges the artwork a touch target for a row with nothing in it.

      Server-side only, and deliberately so. One path still reaches an empty rail
      — a curator previewing a work that passes `display_image?` and whose plate
      then 404s from the museum CDN, so `artwork#rest` hides the zoom and there is
      no keep frame beside it. Curator-only, cosmetic, and it would cost a
      `:has()`-of-a-`:not()` to close. Left, knowingly. %>
  <% keeps = chrome != :preview %>
  <% if painting.display_image? || keeps %>
  <div class="rail">
    <%# Same guard the plate uses, for the same reason. A work failing
        `display_image?` renders no <img> at all, so `artwork#rest` can never hide
        this — that path is an `error` event on an element which does not exist.
        Keep stays: a resting work is still a work you can keep. %>
    <% if painting.display_image? %>
      <%# "Zoom in on", not "View … full screen" — the plate's own button already
          uses that exact sentence, and the two are consecutive tab stops. A
          screen reader user tabbing off the artwork heard the identical name
          twice in a row before reaching Keep. Both names are accurate; only one
          of them may be this one. %>
      <button type="button" class="rail__act"
              data-action="artwork#open" data-artwork-target="railZoom"
              data-artwork-title="<%= painting.title %>"
              aria-label="Zoom in on <%= painting.title %>">
        <%# The plate's own 1px frame, redrawn at 23px: the glyph that opens the
            artwork is a drawing of the artwork's corners. %>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="23" height="23" aria-hidden="true"
             fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"
             stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
          <path d="M4 9.2V4h5.2M20 9.2V4h-5.2M4 14.8V20h5.2M20 14.8V20h-5.2"/>
        </svg>
      </button>
    <% end %>

    <%# The frame's DEFAULT CONTENT: the keep mark, drawn and going nowhere.

        It exists so the rail is not an empty 44px hole where the habit mechanic
        should be for as long as the fetch takes. Below the fold that lag was
        invisible; under the artwork it is the first thing a reader looks at, and
        shipping it would be the bug this story removes, moved from the bottom of
        the page to the top.

        A SPAN, not the real button, and that is the whole subtlety. `button_to`
        emits a CSRF token; generating one starts a session; a session puts a
        `Set-Cookie` on a page that Thruster caches publicly for everyone. Story
        0007 drew that line and `favorites_test.rb` holds it in one assertion. So
        the cached page carries the shape and none of the machinery, and the
        private frame brings the machinery when it lands a moment later.

        `aria-hidden`, because a mark that cannot be pressed should not be
        announced as one — the frame's real button arrives with the accessible
        name that says what it does.

        NOT lazy. It was, back when this frame sat below a 55vh plate plus the
        whole note and a reader who bounced at the picture never paid for it.
        Under the artwork it is inside the viewport on every route, viewport and
        text size `dynamic_type_test.rb` asserts, so `loading: "lazy"` could only
        ever buy an IntersectionObserver that fires immediately — while deferring
        the request past first layout and lengthening the exact window the
        placeholder above exists to cover.

        So it resolves on open for every reader rather than only those who
        scrolled, and the visitor identifier goes with it. Accepted 2026-08-14;
        the App Store privacy label owes that sentence. %>
    <% if keeps %>
      <%= turbo_frame_tag "keep_#{painting.id}", src: favorite_control_path(painting),
            class: "rail__slot" do %>
        <span class="rail__act rail__act--waiting" aria-hidden="true"><%=
          render "favorites/keep_glyph", filled: false %></span>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  </div>
  <% end %>

  <div class="label">
    <h1 class="label__title <%= "label__title--long" if painting.long_title? %>"><%= painting.title %></h1>

    <p class="label__artist">
      <span class="label__artist-name"><%= painting.artist_display %></span>
      <% if painting.life_date.present? %>
        <span class="label__artist-dates"><%= painting.life_date %></span>
      <% end %>
    </p>

    <% if painting.meta_line.present? %>
      <p class="label__meta"><%= painting.meta_line %></p>
    <% end %>

    <%# The curator's note is shown whole — it is the whole point, and it is
        written to a 60-180 word target. Museum copy is not: it runs to 324
        words in this pool, which would push the artwork off a phone screen. So
        borrowed words get the same clamped treatment they get in the archive,
        and they say whose they are. %>
    <% if pick.hand_written? %>
      <div class="label__note" id="daily-note"><%= simple_format(pick.note) %></div>
    <% else %>
      <div class="label__body" id="daily-note" data-controller="expand">
        <p class="label__text" data-expand-target="text"><%= pick.note %></p>
        <button type="button" class="label__more" data-expand-target="button"
                data-action="expand#toggle" hidden>More</button>
        <p class="label__source">From <%= painting.source_name %></p>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <%= tag.p(painting.credit_line, class: "label__credit") if painting.credit_line.present? %>

    <footer class="coda">
      <div class="ornament" aria-hidden="true">✦</div>

      <% if archive %>
        <%= render "days/walk", pick: pick, current: current %>
      <% else %>
        <p class="coda__line">See you tomorrow.</p>
        <a class="caps-link" href="<%= feed_path %>">Wander the full gallery →</a>
      <% end %>
    </footer>

    <%# The sign-in fragment (story 0015) — front door only, and never on the
        curator's preview. Static markup in the cached page, like the keep
        frame above; the state arrives privately. The frame's own id is the
        anchor every wall bounce lands on (`/#signin`), so it lives HERE in
        the public HTML, identical for everyone. Empty by default on purpose:
        the signed-out buttons appear only when the fragment says so, which is
        also how the shell never paints them. %>
    <% if chrome == :front_door %>
      <%= turbo_frame_tag "signin", src: session_control_path, loading: "lazy" %>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <%= render "shared/zoom" %>
</main>
